WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.810
Secure upload workflows start before the file is accepted.

2
00:00:03.810 --> 00:00:14.934
The application should enforce upload size limits, allowed categories, authentication requirements, authorization rules, and rate limits appropriate to the workflow.

3
00:00:14.934 --> 00:00:20.344
Client-side checks improve usability, but server-side validation is the control.

4
00:00:20.344 --> 00:00:27.354
A user who bypasses the browser should not be able to submit an oversized, unsupported, or unauthorized file.

5
00:00:27.354 --> 00:00:31.773
Generated filenames are safer than user-controlled storage names.

6
00:00:31.773 --> 00:00:42.059
The original filename can be stored as metadata for display or audit purposes after validation and encoding, but the storage path should be chosen by the server.

7
00:00:42.059 --> 00:00:51.964
This prevents path manipulation, naming collisions, confusing overwrite behavior, and accidental publication under a meaningful or executable name.

8
00:00:51.964 --> 00:00:56.231
The storage identifier should not reveal sensitive information.

9
00:00:56.231 --> 00:01:04.688
Malware scanning and quarantine patterns can reduce risk where files may later be opened by users or processed by internal systems.

10
00:01:04.688 --> 00:01:08.346
Scanning should be treated as one layer, not a guarantee.

11
00:01:08.346 --> 00:01:18.022
Some workflows benefit from asynchronous processing: upload the file, place it in quarantine, scan and validate it, then mark it ready when accepted.

12
00:01:18.022 --> 00:01:22.670
This avoids forcing complex scanning and parsing into the request path.

13
00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:32.499
Safe upload errors should explain what the user can fix without exposing internal paths, scanner details, stack traces, or sensitive policy internals.

14
00:01:32.499 --> 00:01:40.804
Upload features should also be protected from abuse as cheap storage, data exfiltration staging, or resource exhaustion paths.

15
00:01:40.804 --> 00:01:48.728
A good workflow accounts for size, count, frequency, user identity, tenant context, storage quotas, cleanup, and review.
